Teach 0 A.D.'s own macOS dependency script an iOS mode.

There is no Termux for iOS, so unlike the Android port every dependency has to
be built against the SDK. Writing a new script for that would mean repeating
what libraries/build-macos-libs.sh already does for macOS - download, patch,
configure, install, and collect every .pc file where premake's macosx branch
looks for it (libraries/macos/pkgconfig). So it gets an iOS mode instead.

Set IOS_SDK to iphonesimulator or iphoneos; everything else follows. The script
already had most of the hooks: SYSROOT, ARCH, HOST_PLATFORM for cross builds,
ICU_CROSS_BUILD, LIBSODIUM_SKIP_TESTS, CMAKE_FLAGS. What had to be added:

* **-target instead of -mmacosx-version-min.** Device and simulator are both
  arm64; the only thing that separates them is the target triple, and
  -mmacosx-version-min would fight it.

* **The sysroot in CPPFLAGS, not only in CFLAGS.** A good number of configure
  tests run the preprocessor alone, and without a sysroot those cannot even find
  <sys/types.h>. curl fails in exactly that way, and the message is a masterpiece
  of misdirection: "socket can be linked: yes", "socket is prototyped: no", and
  then, thirty seconds into make, "We can't compile without socket() support!"

* **Everything is a cross build**, even though the runner is arm64 too: a
  simulator binary needs the simulator to run and a device binary needs the
  device, so nothing built here can be executed. That is why ICU_CROSS_BUILD and
  LIBSODIUM_SKIP_TESTS are set regardless of the host architecture, --host is
  passed, and CHOST is exported for zlib, whose hand-written configure otherwise
  guesses from uname. (autoconf ignores CHOST, so it costs nothing elsewhere.)

* **SDL2 through CMake.** Its autotools build has no UIKit backend at all -
  `--enable-video-cocoa` is the desktop one. The CMake build does support iOS.
  Static, like everything else here: an app that is not a framework bundle has
  nowhere sensible to put a dylib.

* **Boost headers only.** 0 A.D. never links Boost on this platform - the macosx
  branch of extern_libs5.lua adds an include path and nothing else, and the
  generated link line carries no -lboost_* - so cross-building filesystem and
  system with b2 would be a long build for nothing.

* **Ten libraries are skipped**, and not because they fail: each is switched off
  in the engine anyway. wxWidgets is Atlas, gmp/nettle/gnutls/gloox are the
  multiplayer lobby, ogg/vorbis/openal are the sound, MoltenVK would be a
  Vulkan backend that needs a renderer port of its own. `ios_skip` says so per
  library instead of silently doing nothing.

The prefix stays libraries/macos, because premake hardcodes it (libraries_dir,
plus the boost and iconv paths). An iOS build therefore overwrites a macOS one
in the same tree, which is fine for CI and worth knowing about by hand.

--- libraries/build-macos-libs.sh
+++ libraries/build-macos-libs.sh
@@ -65,6 +65,36 @@
 export MIN_OSX_VERSION="${MIN_OSX_VERSION:="10.15"}"
 export ARCH="${ARCH:=""}"
 
+# iOS mode: set IOS_SDK to iphonesimulator or iphoneos, everything else follows
+# from it. iOS is Darwin, so this is the same script with a different SDK, a
+# different version flag and one honest consequence: nothing built here can be
+# executed, so every library is a cross build even when the host is also arm64.
+IOS=false
+if [ -n "$IOS_SDK" ]; then
+	IOS=true
+	MIN_IOS_VERSION="${MIN_IOS_VERSION:="13.0"}"
+	case "$IOS_SDK" in
+		iphonesimulator) IOS_TARGET="arm64-apple-ios$MIN_IOS_VERSION-simulator" ;;
+		iphoneos)        IOS_TARGET="arm64-apple-ios$MIN_IOS_VERSION" ;;
+		*) echo "ERROR: IOS_SDK must be iphonesimulator or iphoneos"; exit 1 ;;
+	esac
+	SYSROOT="${SYSROOT:="$(xcrun --sdk "$IOS_SDK" --show-sdk-path)"}"
+	ARCH=arm64
+	# -mmacosx-version-min would fight the -target below.
+	MIN_OSX_VERSION=""
+	export CC="$(xcrun --sdk "$IOS_SDK" -f clang)"
+	export CXX="$(xcrun --sdk "$IOS_SDK" -f clang++)"
+	# zlib's configure is hand written and guesses from uname unless CHOST says
+	# otherwise. autoconf ignores CHOST, so setting it here costs nothing.
+	export CHOST=aarch64-apple-darwin
+	# And the sysroot has to be in CPPFLAGS as well, not only in CFLAGS: a good
+	# number of configure tests run the *preprocessor* alone, and without a
+	# sysroot those cannot even find <sys/types.h>. curl fails in exactly that
+	# way -- "socket can be linked: yes", "socket is prototyped: no", and then
+	# "We can't compile without socket() support!" thirty seconds into make.
+	export CPPFLAGS="$CPPFLAGS -isysroot $SYSROOT -target $IOS_TARGET"
+fi
+
 # The various libs offer inconsistent configure options, some allow
 # setting sysroot and OS X-specific options, others don't. Adding to
 # the confusion, Apple moved /Developer/SDKs into the Xcode app bundle
@@ -84,6 +114,12 @@
 	# and CRT version, and use it to set the macosx_version_min linker flag
 	LDFLAGS="$LDFLAGS -mmacosx-version-min=$MIN_OSX_VERSION"
 fi
+# The -target triple is what separates the device from the simulator: both are
+# arm64, and -arch alone cannot tell them apart.
+if [ "$IOS" = "true" ]; then
+	C_FLAGS="$C_FLAGS -target $IOS_TARGET"
+	LDFLAGS="$LDFLAGS -target $IOS_TARGET"
+fi
 
 CFLAGS="$CFLAGS $C_FLAGS -fvisibility=hidden"
 CXXFLAGS="$CXXFLAGS $C_FLAGS -stdlib=libc++ -std=c++17"
@@ -113,6 +149,16 @@
 	ICU_CROSS_BUILD=true
 	LIBSODIUM_SKIP_TESTS=true
 fi
+if [ "$IOS" = "true" ]; then
+	# Not because the architecture differs -- it usually does not, the runner is
+	# arm64 too -- but because a simulator binary needs the simulator to run and
+	# a device binary needs the device. Anything a configure script wants to
+	# execute is out of reach, so ICU needs its native build first and
+	# libsodium's tests have to stay unbuilt.
+	ICU_CROSS_BUILD=true
+	LIBSODIUM_SKIP_TESTS=true
+	HOST_PLATFORM="--host=aarch64-apple-darwin"
+fi
 
 CFLAGS="$CFLAGS -arch $ARCH"
 CXXFLAGS="$CXXFLAGS -arch $ARCH"
@@ -121,6 +167,10 @@
 
 # CMake doesn't seem to pick up on architecture with CFLAGS only
 CMAKE_FLAGS="-DCMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES=$ARCH -DCMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=$MIN_OSX_VERSION"
+if [ "$IOS" = "true" ]; then
+	CMAKE_FLAGS="-DCMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=iOS -DCMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES=$ARCH \
+		-DCMAKE_OSX_SYSROOT=$IOS_SDK -DCMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=$MIN_IOS_VERSION"
+fi
 
 JOBS=${JOBS:="-j2"}
 
@@ -150,6 +200,17 @@
 	echo "Skipping - already built (use --force-rebuild to override)"
 }
 
+# Libraries that an iOS build does not want. Not "does not work": each of them
+# is switched off in the engine anyway -- wxWidgets is Atlas, gmp/nettle/gnutls
+# /gloox are the multiplayer lobby, ogg/vorbis/openal are the sound, and
+# MoltenVK would be the Vulkan backend, which needs a renderer port of its own.
+ios_skip()
+{
+	[ "$IOS" = "true" ] || return 1
+	echo "Skipping $1 - not built for iOS"
+	return 0
+}
+
 # Check that we're actually on macOS
 if [ "$(uname -s)" != "Darwin" ]; then
 	die "This script is intended for macOS only"
@@ -398,6 +459,19 @@
 
